course: money
lesson: 11
title: Prices and change at the till
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: At the till you pay, and if you gave more than the price, you get change back; doing the sum yourself means you always pay the right amount.
explain: The fastest way to check change is to count up from the price: Jake pays £10 for a £3.50 toy, so £3.50 + 50p = £4, + £1 = £5, + £5 = £10, and his change is 50p + £1 + £5 = £6.50.
explain: Add your prices before you get to the till: a £1.20 drink and 85p crisps make £2.05, which is 5p more than £2, so rounding to "about £1.20 + about 90p" warns you early.
explain: People make mistakes, so be honest both ways: if the new lad gives you £1 too much change, say so, and if the till charges more than the shelf says, point it out politely.
fact: Change is the money you get back when you pay more than the price.
fact: Counting up from the price to the money you gave is a quick way to check change.
fact: To estimate is to make a quick rough guess, for example by rounding prices before adding them.
fact: If you get too much change, the honest thing is to say so; the worker may have to make up the missing money.
fact: Sometimes the shelf price and the till price do not match; you can politely point it out and shops usually fix it.
fact: Being charged too much is called being overcharged.
fact: To find how many things you can afford, see how many times the price fits into your money; what is left is your change.
fact: Different shops charge different prices for the same thing; checking a few shops is called shopping around.
fact: Giving a few extra coins so the change comes out round, like £1.10 for a 60p lolly, means fewer coins for everyone.
fact: Prices go up slowly over the years; this is called inflation, which is why grown-ups remember cheaper prices.
fact: Market sellers often say a rounded price out loud, so it is worth doing the sum yourself.
fact: "Fair cop" is British slang for admitting you were caught out fairly.
exercise: Ollie pays for a £2.60 comic with a £5 note. What change should he get? | £2.40 | £3.40 | £2.60 | £1.40
exercise: The new lad gave Ollie £3.40. How much too much is that? | £1 | 80p | £1.40 | 40p
exercise: What should Ollie do with the extra change? | Tell the lad, because it's the honest thing | Keep it, it's the shop's mistake | Spend it quickly before anyone notices | Give it to a friend
exercise: Mia wants a £1.20 drink and 85p crisps. What is the total? | £2.05 | £1.95 | £2.15 | £2.00
exercise: Mia has £2. Does she have enough? | No, she is 5p short | Yes, with 5p change left over | Yes, it is exactly right | No, she is 15p short
exercise: A Freddo used to be 10p and now costs 25p. How much more is it? | 15p | 25p | 35p | 10p
exercise: The shelf says 80p but the till charges £1.10. How much too much is that? | 30p | 20p | 40p | £1.90
exercise: Jake pays £10 for a £3.50 toy. What change does he get? | £6.50 | £7.50 | £6.00 | £5.50
exercise: Sofia buys 3 apples at 30p each. What should she pay? | 90p | £1.00 | 60p | 33p
exercise: Archie has 75p. Sweets are 20p each. How many can he buy? | 3 | 4 | 2 | 5
exercise: After buying 3 sweets at 20p each, how much does Archie have left? | 15p | 5p | 25p | 35p
exercise: A tag says £4 and the shop over the road sells it for £3. How much cheaper over the road? | £1 | £7 | £3 | 50p
exercise: Ayla pays £1.10 for a 60p lolly. What change does she get? | 50p | 40p | 60p | £1.70
exercise: Why does the shopkeeper ask Ayla for the extra 10p? | So the change comes out as one round coin | Because the lolly really costs 70p | To keep the 10p as a tip | Because 10p coins are rare
exercise: Kieran buys 2 cans at 65p and crisps at 70p. What is the total? | £2.00 | £1.95 | £2.05 | £1.35
exercise: Kieran has £2. How much change does he get? | None | 5p | 10p | 65p
exercise: What is shopping around? | Checking prices in a few shops before buying | Walking round and round a shop without buying anything | Buying everything you need in just one big shop | Running round the shop aisles with friends just for fun
test: Mate at the till: "it's £2.60 and I gave a fiver, he's given me £3.40, result!" What's the honest move? | Tell the lad it's £1 too much | Keep it, it's his fault not yours | Ask him for even more change back | Leave the shop as fast as you can
test: Market man: "three apples at 30p, that's a pound, love." Sofia says "isn't it 90p?" He laughs: "fair cop." ("Fair cop" means you caught me fairly.) Who was right? | Sofia: 3 × 30p = 90p | The man: it's £1 | Both: 90p rounds to £1 so it's the same | Neither: it's 60p
test: Bloke in the newsagent's: "25p for a Freddo?? Daylight robbery!" ("Daylight robbery" means a price that feels like stealing.) Why did the price go up? | Prices slowly rise over the years; that's inflation | The shop is stealing from him | Freddos got bigger | The bloke is just bad at maths
test: Archie goes "75p, sweets 20p, so I get 4, easy." What's wrong? | 4 sweets cost 80p, which is 5p more than he has | Nothing, 4 sweets is right | He can actually buy 5 | He can only buy 2
test: Jake hands over a tenner for a £3.50 toy and the man gives him a fiver and a £1 coin. What's missing? | 50p, the change should be £6.50 | Nothing, £6 is right | £1, the change should be £7 | £1.50, the change should be £7.50
test: Callum's mate: "in the shop over the road it's £3, bro." Callum's buying 2. How much does he save over the road? | £2 | £1 | £6 | £8
test: What would happen if nobody ever checked their change? | Mistakes and tricks would go unnoticed and people would lose money | Shops would always give too much change | Change would become free | Nothing, tills are never wrong
test: Kieran: "2 cans of pop at 65p and crisps at 70p — no wait, 75p." He has £2. Can he buy all three? | No, it's £2.05, 5p short | Yes, it's exactly £2 | Yes, with 5p change | No, it's £2.20
test: A Kiwi kid says "sweet as, the dairy's got Jaffas for 40c, I'll get 3." ("Sweet as" means great; a "dairy" is a corner shop in NZ.) What do 3 cost? | $1.20 | $1.40 | $1.00 | $0.12
test: The till says £1.10 for juice; the shelf says 80p. Mum says "Excuse me, the shelf says 80p." What usually happens? | The shop checks and fixes the price | She is told to leave the shop at once | She pays £1.90 for both of the prices | Nothing, because shelf prices don't count
test: Ayla's got a £1 coin and a 10p. Lolly's 60p. She gives the lot. What's her change? | One 50p coin | Four 10p coins | 40p | 60p
exam: A purchaser tenders a five-pound note in respect of a periodical priced at two pounds sixty pence. Ascertain the change due. | Two pounds forty pence | Three pounds forty pence | Two pounds sixty pence | One pound forty pence
exam: It is given that a beverage is priced at one pound twenty pence and a packet of crisps at eighty-five pence. Determine whether a sum of two pounds suffices for the purchase of both. | It does not, being insufficient by five pence | It does, leaving five pence as change | It does, precisely and without remainder | It does not, being insufficient by fifteen pence
exam: A sum of seventy-five pence is held and confectionery is priced at twenty pence per item. State the greatest number of items which may be purchased. | Three | Four | Two | Five
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with honesty where change in excess of the correct amount is received. | Informing the cashier of the excess | Retaining the excess without any remark | Departing the premises with expedition | Requesting a further sum from the cashier
